<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David Friedrich Strauss</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a>–<a href="/years/1874">1874</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">David Friedrich Strauss was a German theologian and writer from Ludwigsburg, a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-christian-baur">Ferdinand Christian Baur</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>. His Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> (1835–1836) treated the gospel miracles as myths shaped by early Christian belief, caused a sensation and cost him his academic career. It became a founding work of the historical study of Jesus and was translated into English by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-eliot">George Eliot</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-friedrich-strauss.jpg" alt="David Friedrich Strauss" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hermann Günther · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Friedrich_Strauss_BW.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Strauss was born in Ludwigsburg, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, on 27 January 1808. At twelve he was sent to the evangelical seminary at Blaubeuren, where his masters Friedrich Heinrich Kern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-christian-baur">Ferdinand Christian Baur</a> taught their pupils to appreciate the ancient classics and the principles of textual criticism, which could be applied to sacred texts as to classical ones. In 1825 he entered the Tübinger Stift. He later taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, history and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> at Maulbronn and Blaubeuren, and in October 1831 went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> to study under Schleiermacher and Hegel, though Hegel died just as he arrived. In the autumn of 1833 he resigned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> post to finish his book on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-life-of-jesus-critically-examined">Das Leben Jesu</a>, kritisch bearbeitet appeared in 1835 and 1836, when he was 27, and was a sensation. Without denying that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> existed, Strauss argued that the miracles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> were mythical additions with little basis in fact. By myth he meant not mere falsity but the imaginative expression of religious truth. He held that the early church developed the stories to present Jesus as the Messiah of Jewish prophecy. This was a third way between rationalism, which explained miracles as misread natural events, and the supernaturalist view that the accounts were entirely accurate.</p><p>The book brought fierce controversy. When Strauss was elected to a chair of theology at the University of Zurich, the storm was so great that the authorities pensioned him before he began, and effigies of him were burnt at the Shrove Tuesday festival. He gave the pension of 1000 Swiss francs a year to the poor. In 1840 and 1841 he published Christliche Glaubenslehre, which argued that the history of Christian doctrines was the history of their disintegration. He then left theology for over twenty years and wrote biographies. His last work, Der alte und der neue Glaube (1872), caused almost as great a sensation, since friends saw in it a one-sided view of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and a turn from spiritual philosophy to materialism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Strauss died in Ludwigsburg on 8 February 1874. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-eliot">George Eliot</a> translated his Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> into English in 1846. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-schweitzer">Albert Schweitzer</a> wrote in The Quest of the Historical Jesus that research fell into two periods, before and after David Strauss. Marcus Borg has suggested that the details of Strauss's argument have not had a long-lasting effect, but that his basic claims, that many gospel narratives are mythical in character and that myth is not the same as falsehood, have become part of mainstream scholarship.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “David Strauss” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The University of Tübingen and its Protestant seminary trained clergy, officials and scholars, and the theologian Ferdinand Christian Baur and his pupil <b>David Friedrich Strauss</b>, whose Life of Jesus of 1835 caused a sensation, made the Tübingen school central to modern biblical criticism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ludwigsburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Ludwigsburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.9,9.19&amp;year=1850" aria-label="Ludwigsburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4762 457 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of David Friedrich Strauss"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.9,9.19&amp;year=1850">What was here in 1850? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
